# The spinor double cover, modeled by the kernel, importing the shared `sign` atom. A SPINOR is a state that needs a full
# DOUBLE turn (4 pi) to return to itself: a single 2 pi rotation does not bring it back, it flips its sign to negative.
# This is the defining mark of half-integer spin, and the rotation group is doubly covered by the spin group exactly this
# way. The unit quaternions realize it: i squared is minus one, so a 2 pi turn about any axis is the element minus one,
# and minus one squared is plus one, so 4 pi returns. Here it is modeled on the sign a spinor carries: a 2 pi turn flips
# the sign, two of them return, and a 2 pi turn is genuinely NOT the identity (the spinor sign distinguishes them).

load @term/seed/code/atom/sign
  find sign
  find flip-sign
  find positive
  find negative

# A 2 pi (full) turn flips the spinor's sign: positive becomes negative. A spinor rotated once is minus itself.
rule full-turn-flips-the-sign
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call flip-sign
        make positive
      make negative
  calm hold

# A 4 pi (double) turn returns the spinor to itself: flipping the sign twice is the identity, for every sign. Only after
# two full turns is a spinor back where it started, the hallmark of spin one-half.
rule double-turn-returns
  mark s, like sign
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call flip-sign
        call flip-sign
          read s
      read s
  fold s

# A 2 pi turn is NOT the identity: positive and negative are genuinely different, so a single full rotation changes the
# spinor's state. This distinguishes a spinor from an ordinary vector (which 2 pi returns), the topological fact behind
# the electron's minus sign under a full turn. Shown by refutation.
rule full-turn-is-not-trivial
  show miss
    call is-equal
      make positive
      make negative
  calm miss
